WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.222 --> 00:00:17.428
A set of double doors opened at the rear of the dining room, and Captain Nemo led me

2
00:00:17.468 --> 00:00:18.209
into the next room.

3
00:00:19.009 --> 00:00:19.850
It was a library.

4
00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:25.153
Tall, black bookcases lined the walls, and they held a huge number of books.

5
00:00:26.173 --> 00:00:30.036
At the base of the bookcases were comfortable-looking couches covered in brown leather.

6
00:00:30.996 --> 00:00:35.659
In the center of the room stood an immense table littered with pamphlets and old newspapers.

7
00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:40.923
Captain Nemo stretched out on a couch while I continued to admire his library.

8
00:00:40.943 --> 00:00:45.386
"This library belongs in a fine European palace," I said.

9
00:00:45.406 --> 00:00:49.989
"I'm absolutely astounded when I consider that it's at the bottom of the sea."

10
00:00:50.009 --> 00:00:55.813
"Where could one find greater solitude or silence than under the sea?"

11
00:00:56.233 --> 00:00:56.973
replied the captain.

12
00:00:56.993 --> 00:01:00.756
"Was your study at the museum as perfectly quiet as this?"

13
00:01:01.876 --> 00:01:02.316
"No, sir.

14
00:01:02.696 --> 00:01:04.897
You must have six or seven thousand books here."

15
00:01:04.917 --> 00:01:09.058
"Actually twelve thousand," said Captain Nemo.

16
00:01:09.078 --> 00:01:14.459
"I finished with the world on the day the Nautilus plunged beneath the waves for the first time.

17
00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:19.441
On that day I bought my last books and newspapers, and I like to think that people have

18
00:01:19.481 --> 00:01:20.801
written nothing more since then.

19
00:01:21.181 --> 00:01:23.282
Please feel free to make use of this library."

20
00:01:24.282 --> 00:01:26.503
I began to examine the volumes on his shelves.

21
00:01:27.503 --> 00:01:34.830
With books in every language, his collection included works on science, ethics, and literature but nothing on politics

22
00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:35.551
or economics.

23
00:01:36.571 --> 00:01:39.714
By far the greatest number of books was about science.

24
00:01:40.754 --> 00:01:45.558
I saw a fairly new astronomy book that I knew had been published in 1864.

25
00:01:46.518 --> 00:01:51.382
From that, I concluded that the captain had been living underwater for at most three years.

26
00:01:51.402 --> 00:01:56.425
"Come, let's continue our tour," said the captain, rising from the couch.

27
00:01:57.906 --> 00:02:02.353
A door opened in the wall opposite where we had entered the room, and I followed Captain Nemo

28
00:02:02.413 --> 00:02:03.634
into a spacious room.

29
00:02:04.554 --> 00:02:10.417
The splendid lighting revealed that this room was a large gallery filled with treasures from the natural and

30
00:02:10.497 --> 00:02:11.358
artistic worlds.

31
00:02:12.498 --> 00:02:17.621
The walls were adorned with more than thirty paintings by some of the most famous artists of the

32
00:02:17.681 --> 00:02:18.822
past and present.

33
00:02:19.702 --> 00:02:25.205
The old masters were represented by Raphael, da Vinci, Rubens, and many others.

34
00:02:26.126 --> 00:02:30.028
In the corners of the room stood some admirable statues in marble and bronze.

35
00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:37.102
"Sir," I said, "without seeking to know who you are, I recognize that you are an artist."

36
00:02:38.162 --> 00:02:41.083
"No, merely a collector," said Captain Nemo.

37
00:02:41.103 --> 00:02:45.664
"I used to love to collect these beautiful works created by the hands of man.

38
00:02:46.664 --> 00:02:51.226
I greedily sought them out, and I've been able to bring together some objects of great value.

39
00:02:52.126 --> 00:02:55.687
But now they are my last reminders of a world that is dead to me."

40
00:02:57.207 --> 00:03:01.273
He seemed to go into a daydream and forget that I was there, so I took the opportunity

41
00:03:01.313 --> 00:03:02.434
to study the rest of the room.

42
00:03:03.515 --> 00:03:08.999
The next thing I noticed was a large organ upon which were strewn works by Mozart, Beethoven, and

43
00:03:09.119 --> 00:03:10.000
other great musicians.

44
00:03:11.041 --> 00:03:15.605
In the center of the room was a fountain with a single stream of water that rose into

45
00:03:15.645 --> 00:03:16.005
the air.

46
00:03:16.786 --> 00:03:18.947
The base was made from a giant clamshell.

47
00:03:19.908 --> 00:03:24.532
Around this fountain stood elegant glass cases filled with wonders from the sea.

48
00:03:25.473 --> 00:03:26.934
You can imagine my interest in those.

49
00:03:27.915 --> 00:03:31.009
One case was filled with coral, another with sponges.

50
00:03:31.869 --> 00:03:35.512
Yet another contained starfish, sea urchins, and sea cucumbers.

51
00:03:36.533 --> 00:03:41.296
Anyone interested in shells would have fainted at the sight of cases filled with so many rare and

52
00:03:41.356 --> 00:03:43.497
expensive specimens from around the globe.

53
00:03:44.418 --> 00:03:52.923
Another case was devoted solely to pearls: pink pearls, green pearls, yellow, blue, and black pearls, the unusual

54
00:03:52.983 --> 00:03:55.745
production of diverse oysters from every ocean.

55
00:03:57.286 --> 00:03:59.648
Some of these pearls were larger than birds' eggs!

56
00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:03.361
It was impossible to estimate the value of this collection.

57
00:04:04.282 --> 00:04:08.623
Captain Nemo must have spent millions to acquire these various natural specimens.

58
00:04:09.544 --> 00:04:13.665
I was wondering where his money came from when he interrupted my thoughts.

59
00:04:13.685 --> 00:04:17.247
"You're examining my shells and pearls, Professor?"

60
00:04:17.627 --> 00:04:17.947
he said.

61
00:04:17.967 --> 00:04:23.649
"They must be of interest to a naturalist like yourself, but they have a special significance to me

62
00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:26.190
because I collected them all myself."

63
00:04:27.451 --> 00:04:30.556
"No museum in Europe possesses such a collection," I said.

64
00:04:30.576 --> 00:04:35.721
"But if I exhaust all my admiration on it, I won't have any left for the Nautilus itself.

65
00:04:36.681 --> 00:04:40.865
I don't wish to pry into your secrets, but I confess I'd like to know more about the

66
00:04:40.905 --> 00:04:45.529
submarine. I see instruments hanging on these walls that I'm not familiar with."

67
00:04:46.489 --> 00:04:51.273
"You'll find these same instruments in my own cabin, where I'll explain them to you," said the captain.

68
00:04:51.293 --> 00:04:54.656
"But first come look at your own cabin."

69
00:04:55.597 --> 00:05:00.873
He led me out of the gallery, through a passage, and into my cabin, which was really too

70
00:05:01.053 --> 00:05:02.635
elegant to be called a cabin.

71
00:05:03.596 --> 00:05:07.759
It was furnished with a bed, table, and several other pieces of furniture.

72
00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:10.201
I found myself thanking my host.

73
00:05:10.221 --> 00:05:15.526
"Your room adjoins mine, which opens into the gallery," Captain Nemo explained.

74
00:05:16.467 --> 00:05:21.071
We entered the captain's room, which had a severe, almost monk-like appearance to it.

75
00:05:21.971 --> 00:05:26.976
There was only a small bed and a table—no comforts, only the strictest necessities.

76
00:05:27.968 --> 00:05:30.250
Captain Nemo pointed to a seat and I sat down.

77
00:05:31.211 --> 00:05:36.395
He explained that here and in the gallery, he had all the instruments that he needed to navigate

78
00:05:36.435 --> 00:05:38.397
the sub and keep track of the weather.

79
00:05:38.417 --> 00:05:41.319
"But how do you power everything?"

80
00:05:41.759 --> 00:05:42.220
I asked.

81
00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:44.722
"Ah," said Captain Nemo.

82
00:05:44.742 --> 00:05:53.129
"There is something that reigns supreme onboard my vessel: this agent lights the submarine, warms it, and is

83
00:05:53.169 --> 00:05:55.230
the soul of my mechanical apparatus.

84
00:05:56.271 --> 00:05:58.244
This agent is electricity."

85
00:05:58.265 --> 00:06:01.346
"Electricity!" I cried in surprise.

86
00:06:02.086 --> 00:06:07.648
On land electricity was able to produce only limited amounts of power; it wasn't capable of powering such

87
00:06:07.668 --> 00:06:10.209
a fast and complicated vessel as the Nautilus.

88
00:06:10.229 --> 00:06:12.710
"But how are you able to do this?

89
00:06:13.150 --> 00:06:17.512
The batteries that you use to generate this marvelous force must be depleted very quickly."

90
00:06:17.532 --> 00:06:23.915
"That's true, but my batteries are powered by sodium and someday I'll show you how we mine that."

91
00:06:24.835 --> 00:06:26.876
"Do the batteries produce fresh air for the sub?"

92
00:06:27.260 --> 00:06:27.580
I asked.

93
00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:33.283
"We could do that and we have some reservoirs filled with air, but I prefer to rise to

94
00:06:33.323 --> 00:06:35.645
the surface every day to replenish our air supply."

95
00:06:35.665 --> 00:06:42.749
"Aha!" I said, for now I understood the sounds we'd heard onboard the Abraham Lincoln.

96
00:06:43.669 --> 00:06:48.292
The Nautilus really did rise to the surface of the ocean to breathe like a whale.

97
00:06:49.232 --> 00:06:52.854
We continued our tour and the captain showed me the engine room and the kitchen.

98
00:06:53.835 --> 00:06:58.161
The door to the crew's quarters was closed, so I couldn't tell how many men were onboard.

99
00:06:59.081 --> 00:07:03.823
He also showed me a small boat that could be released from the sub for fishing or pleasure.

100
00:07:04.704 --> 00:07:06.344
But I was still filled with questions.

101
00:07:06.364 --> 00:07:09.966
"How does the sub descend to great depths?"

102
00:07:10.486 --> 00:07:10.886
I asked.

103
00:07:10.906 --> 00:07:13.287
"How does it rise back to the surface?

104
00:07:13.767 --> 00:07:15.868
I hope I'm not asking too many questions."

105
00:07:16.849 --> 00:07:19.270
"I don't mind answering," replied Captain Nemo.

106
00:07:20.170 --> 00:07:23.191
He stared at me for a moment before adding a grim reminder.

107
00:07:23.211 --> 00:07:28.692
"Because you'll never be able to leave the Nautilus to tell anyone what you've seen."
